## The script is the clip

Each clip is a TypeScript file in the repository. The video beside it is what CI recorded from that file on the last push to `main`, and the lit line is the one running.

<Take base="{{clips}}" />

## What happens when you change the app

<Ledger />

Every push to `main` runs `npx versioncam check` against the example app. On the left, the last run. On the right, the same check run once for this page, after one button's test id was renamed and nothing else.

<Runs base="{{clips}}" />

## Why a take repeats

<dl class="vc-facts not-content">
  <div>
    <dt>Authored time</dt>
    <dd>The page's clock moves one frame at a time, and only when the recorder moves it. A slow query or a spinner is waited for off camera and never reaches the video. <a href="/how-it-works/overview/">How it works</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Real input</dt>
    <dd>A real mouse follows a planned, seeded path, so hover and press states fire as they would for a person. The cursor you see is drawn afterwards, at output resolution. <a href="/reference/dsl/">The clip DSL</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Same bytes</dt>
    <dd>Two recordings of a clip on one machine are the same file. <code>versioncam stability</code> proves your app does its part before a clip is written for it. <a href="/how-it-works/determinism/">Determinism</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>One sentence</dt>
    <dd>In Claude Code, <code>/versioncam "…"</code> writes the clip, records it, and has a fresh reviewer judge the frames. There is no API key: the model is the session you already have. <a href="/start/authoring/">Authoring</a></dd>
  </div>
</dl>

## After CI: version.cam

The recorder and the GitHub Action are free and run in your CI, which already has your app and your secrets. version.cam is what happens to a recording after CI has made it.

<Hosted />

## Set up

Three commands and one sentence. The first run of the skill writes the config from what it finds in the repository, proves the app records the same way twice, and writes the first clip. On the example app that took [six to twelve minutes and one or two review rounds](/start/authoring/).

